| Secondary text | `color.text.muted` | `#475569` | `#94a3b8` |

Use a semantic role such as “text on primary,” not the action color itself, for a button label. Validate the actual pairing, component size, disabled state, focus state, and selected brand. Color must not be the only signal of status.

### Android · generated color roles

`core/design/theme/EnterpriseTheme.kt`

```kotlin
package com.enterprise.mobile.core.design.theme

import androidx.compose.foundation.isSystemInDarkTheme
import androidx.compose.material3.MaterialTheme
import androidx.compose.material3.darkColorScheme
import androidx.compose.material3.lightColorScheme
import androidx.compose.runtime.Composable
import androidx.compose.ui.graphics.Color

// Representative generated output. Edit the source tokens, not these values.
private val LightColors = lightColorScheme(
    primary = Color(0xFF007A3D), onPrimary = Color(0xFFFFFFFF),
    background = Color(0xFFF4F8F5), onBackground = Color(0xFF0F172A),
    surface = Color(0xFFFFFFFF), onSurface = Color(0xFF0F172A),
    onSurfaceVariant = Color(0xFF475569), outline = Color(0xFFD4E2D8),
)
private val DarkColors = darkColorScheme(
    primary = Color(0xFF22C55E), onPrimary = Color(0xFF09130E),
    background = Color(0xFF09130E), onBackground = Color(0xFFF1F5F9),
    surface = Color(0xFF0D1912), onSurface = Color(0xFFF1F5F9),
    onSurfaceVariant = Color(0xFF94A3B8), outline = Color(0xFF193828),
)

@Composable
fun EnterpriseTheme(
    darkTheme: Boolean = isSystemInDarkTheme(),
    content: @Composable () -> Unit,
) {
    MaterialTheme(
        colorScheme = if (darkTheme) DarkColors else LightColors,
        content = content,
    )
}
```

This color-role example leaves other Material roles at their defaults. A published enterprise theme must also define the roles it uses for errors, containers, typography, shapes, and component states. Product code reads `MaterialTheme` roles or approved semantic extensions instead of copying these literals.

### iOS · adaptive named assets

Generate the named color assets below with light/dark variants in the owning asset catalog. Supply that catalog's bundle explicitly when it is outside the application bundle.

`Core/Design/EnterpriseTheme.swift`

```swift
import Foundation
import SwiftUI

struct EnterpriseTheme: ViewModifier {
    let resourceBundle: Bundle

    func body(content: Content) -> some View {
        content
            .tint(Color("foundation.primary", bundle: resourceBundle))
            .foregroundStyle(Color("foundation.textPrimary", bundle: resourceBundle))
            .background(Color("foundation.background", bundle: resourceBundle))
    }
}

extension View {
    func enterpriseTheme(resourceBundle: Bundle = .main) -> some View {
        modifier(EnterpriseTheme(resourceBundle: resourceBundle))
    }
}
```

The named assets adapt to the environment's appearance. Create the exact asset names referenced by the code; a missing asset is an integration error. For Swift Package resources, pass the package's resource bundle from inside the owning package. The app shell separately owns full-screen backgrounds and safe-area behavior.

### React Native · Redux-backed preferences

`src/core/foundations/foundationSlice.ts`

```typescript
import { createSlice, type PayloadAction } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';

export type ThemePreference = 'system' | 'light' | 'dark';
export type SupportedLanguage = 'en' | 'ar';
export type ApprovedBrandId = 'core' | 'partner';

export interface FoundationPreferences {
  theme: ThemePreference;
  language: SupportedLanguage | 'system';
  brand: ApprovedBrandId;
}

const initialState: FoundationPreferences = {
  theme: 'system', language: 'system', brand: 'core',
};

const foundationSlice = createSlice({
  name: 'foundation',
  initialState,
  reducers: {
    themeChanged(state, action: PayloadAction<ThemePreference>) {
      state.theme = action.payload;
    },
    languageChanged(state, action: PayloadAction<FoundationPreferences['language']>) {
      state.language = action.payload;
    },
    brandChanged(state, action: PayloadAction<ApprovedBrandId>) {
      state.brand = action.payload;
    },
    accountBrandReset(state) {
      state.brand = initialState.brand;
    },
  },
});

export const { themeChanged, languageChanged, brandChanged, accountBrandReset } = foundationSlice.actions;
export const foundationReducer = foundationSlice.reducer;
```

Register `foundationReducer` under `foundation` in the existing root store and update the application's inferred state types. Draft assignments inside `createSlice` are valid Immer updates. Persist only preferences that the product intends to retain; an account brand must not accidentally carry into another account.

`src/core/foundations/theme/ThemeProvider.tsx`

```tsx
import { createContext, useContext, useMemo, type ReactNode } from 'react';
import { useColorScheme } from 'react-native';
import { useAppSelector } from '../../../app/hooks';

type Mode = 'light' | 'dark';
export interface ThemePalette {
  readonly primary: string;
  readonly onPrimary: string;
  readonly background: string;
  readonly surface: string;
  readonly border: string;
  readonly textPrimary: string;
  readonly textMuted: string;
}
export type ThemeTokens = Readonly<Record<Mode, ThemePalette>>;

const ThemeContext = createContext<ThemePalette | null>(null);

// tokens is the complete, generated token set for the currently resolved brand.
export function ThemeProvider({ tokens, children }: Readonly<{
  tokens: ThemeTokens;
  children: ReactNode;
}>) {
  const preference = useAppSelector((state) => state.foundation.theme);
  const system = useColorScheme();
  const mode: Mode = preference === 'system'
    ? (system === 'dark' ? 'dark' : 'light') : preference;
  const palette = useMemo(() => tokens[mode], [tokens, mode]);
  return <ThemeContext.Provider value={palette}>{children}</ThemeContext.Provider>;
}

export function useAppTheme(): ThemePalette {
  const palette = useContext(ThemeContext);
  if (!palette) throw new Error('ThemeProvider is required.');
  return palette;
}
```

<Aside type="tip" title="Hint · Context distributes derived values; Redux owns preferences">
Theme mode and selected brand are application preferences in Redux Toolkit. The theme provider distributes the resolved palette; it does not maintain a second theme preference. Missing providers fail explicitly instead of silently rendering a default brand. Validate persisted/remote preference values before dispatch—TypeScript types do not validate runtime input.
</Aside>

**Review:** No raw visual values in feature components; generated token files may contain literals. Every published token needs the supported variants and meaningful fallbacks. Confirm which rules are actually enforced in CI; code review remains necessary for visual and accessibility behavior.

