## Localization & Internationalization

**Responsibility:** Resolve a supported language, load native translation resources, format values correctly, and apply the appropriate layout direction.

Resolve language in this order: **supported explicit user preference → supported system-language match → documented fallback**. Persist a language tag or the choice “system,” not translated strings or formatted amounts.

| Concern | Android | iOS | React Native |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Resources | `strings.xml`, plurals, localized resources | String Catalogs / localized resources | Versioned i18next resources or approved localization SDK |
| Per-app language | Platform/AppCompat integration for supported versions | System app-language settings or app-owned locale environment where supported | Foundation coordinates Redux preference and i18n engine |
| Number/date formatting | Locale-aware platform formatters | Foundation `FormatStyle` / locale-aware formatters | Supported `Intl` or approved formatter integration |
| Layout direction | Resolved resource/layout configuration | SwiftUI layout direction and localized content | Native RTL configuration and component behavior |

Plural resources and message syntax differ across systems. Do not assume native resource formats and i18next all accept the same ICU syntax without an installed, configured adapter. Never concatenate translated fragments to form a sentence.

### Android · supported per-app language

```kotlin
package com.enterprise.mobile.core.localization

import androidx.annotation.MainThread
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatDelegate
import androidx.core.os.LocaleListCompat

object AppLanguage {
    private val supported = setOf("en", "ar")

    @MainThread
    fun apply(languageTag: String?) {
        require(languageTag == null || languageTag in supported)
        val locales = if (languageTag == null) {
            LocaleListCompat.getEmptyLocaleList() // Follow system language.
        } else {
            LocaleListCompat.forLanguageTags(languageTag)
        }
        AppCompatDelegate.setApplicationLocales(locales)
    }
}
```

Configure the language manifest/resources and persistence integration for your supported Android versions. Backward-compatible AppCompat behavior depends on the host/context setup, particularly on Android 12 and earlier. Language changes may recreate UI; preserve appropriate state rather than promising no recreation. Read direction from the actual resource/layout environment, not the first character of a locale's display name. See [Android per-app language guidance](https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/resources/app-languages).

### iOS · app-owned SwiftUI locale boundary

```swift
import Foundation
import SwiftUI

struct AppLocaleBoundary<Content: View>: View {
    let languageTag: String? // nil means follow system; validate supported tags upstream.
    @Environment(\.locale) private var inheritedLocale
    private let content: Content

    init(languageTag: String?, @ViewBuilder content: () -> Content) {
        self.languageTag = languageTag
        self.content = content()
    }

    var body: some View {
        content.environment(
            \.locale,
            languageTag.map { Locale(identifier: $0) } ?? inheritedLocale
        )
    }
}
```

This sets locale for descendant environment-aware SwiftUI content. It does not change every framework, server response, precomputed string, or native surface. Implement and test layout direction explicitly where the application's language-switching design requires it. Do not mutate `AppleLanguages` with `UserDefaults.synchronize()` as a substitute for a supported localization strategy.

### React Native · Redux preference, one i18n owner

Use `foundation.language` from the Redux slice as the serialized preference. A single application-level coordinator resolves it, awaits the i18n engine's language change, publishes readiness, and handles failures. Do not create a separate independent language preference in a Context provider.

```typescript
export type LanguageTag = 'en' | 'ar';
export type LanguagePreference = LanguageTag | 'system';

export function resolveLanguage(
  preference: LanguagePreference,
  systemTags: readonly string[],
): LanguageTag {
  if (preference !== 'system') return preference;
  for (const tag of systemTags) {
    const base = tag.toLowerCase().split('-')[0];
    if (base === 'en' || base === 'ar') return base;
  }
  return 'en';
}
```

This demonstrates language-only fallback. Apps supporting regional variants need a richer supported-tag negotiation policy. Validate stored preferences and device-language inputs before use. Serialize or version asynchronous language-change requests so an older completion cannot override a newer selection.

<Aside type="tip" title="Hint · text language and native RTL direction are different concerns">
Changing i18next language can update subscribed text without restarting the app. React Native `I18nManager` direction changes are native configuration changes; they take effect on a later application start, and `forceRTL` is primarily a testing mechanism. Do not promise immediate global RTL switching. Define and communicate the supported restart/recreation behavior. See the [I18nManager reference](https://reactnative.dev/docs/i18nmanager).
</Aside>

### Formatting and layout rules

- Keep money as a decimal value or appropriate minor-unit representation according to the domain contract. Do not make binary floating-point the universal financial model.
- Carry a currency code explicitly; language alone does not identify the currency. Format amounts and dates at the presentation boundary.
- Test zero/one/many and language-specific plural categories, long translations, non-Latin digits, and large text.
- Use logical start/end layout. Mirror directional navigation where appropriate, not logos, media, or every icon indiscriminately.
- Keep identifiers, URLs, and mixed-direction text readable; test their bidirectional rendering deliberately.

